PAIRS = [
(r"""The resulting impact on the signal yield is 1\% for the electron efficiency and below 1\% for the muon and photon efficiencies, the latter two being quoted as 0\% at the precision of Table~\ref{tab:uncertainty_summary}. The impact of the conversion-safe electron veto efficiency is 0--2\%.""",
 r"""The resulting impact on the signal yield is 1\% for the electron efficiency and below 1\% for the muon and photon efficiencies, the latter two being quoted as 0\% at the precision of Table~\ref{tab:uncertainty_summary}. The conversion-safe electron veto uncertainty changes the signal yield of the corresponding era by 5--7\%, 4--5\%, 6--8\%, 9--12\% and below 1\% in 2022preEE, 2022postEE, 2023preBPix, 2023postBPix and 2024, respectively, depending on the mass point; since each of the five nuisance parameters affects only one era, its impact on the total signal yield is smaller, 2--3\% when averaged over the eras with their signal yields."""),
(r"""Training variables are reweighted before inputting into the MVA classifier. To account for the uncertainties in the modeling of these variables, their uncertainties must be propagated to the final results. This uncertainty is evaluated by varying each reweighting correction weight by 100\% of its reweighting value, i.e. taking the full difference between the reweighted and un-reweighted templates as the systematic. The full size of the correction, rather than an estimate of its residual uncertainty after reweighting, is used because the reweighted simulation is employed to train the BDT: assigning the full correction as its uncertainty conservatively propagates the effect of the initial imperfect Drell--Yan modeling through the BDT-based selection. The same full-size (100\%) shape-correction prescription is adopted for the photon-identification-BDT shape reweighting in the CMS $H\to Z\gamma$ analysis of the full Run~2 and 2022--2023 data (\href{https://cmsfence.cern.ch/alcm/note/show-note-details?noteID=CMS\%20AN-2022\%2F027}{CMS AN-2022/027}, Section~3.3), where the entire shape correction is likewise taken as its own uncertainty. In that analysis the shape-reweighting uncertainty was initially proposed at 50\%, but was subsequently required by the EGamma POG to be taken at the full 100\%. The associated changes in event yield are used to implement a single nuisance parameter in the statistical model. The effect of the reweighting uncertainty on the signal yield is 1--3\%, as listed in Table~\ref{tab:uncertainty_summary}.""",
 r"""The BDT input variables of the simulation are reweighted in the $m_{\ell\ell\gamma\gamma}$ sideband before the BDT training (Section~\ref{ssec:mcweights}). The reweighting enters only the training: the signal yield used in the statistical model is computed without it, and the reweighting affects the result only through the BDT response, i.e.\ through the signal efficiency after the BDT working point. The corresponding uncertainty is evaluated by deriving the reweighting in three different control regions: the nominal $m_{\ell\ell\gamma\gamma}$ sideband ($95<m_{\ell\ell\gamma\gamma}<115~\GeV$ or $135<m_{\ell\ell\gamma\gamma}<180~\GeV$), and two regions that in addition require the dilepton mass to lie away from the Z boson peak, $50<m_{\ell\ell}<80~\GeV$ or $100<m_{\ell\ell}<120~\GeV$, and $80<m_{\ell\ell}<86~\GeV$ or $96<m_{\ell\ell}<102~\GeV$, respectively. Both alternative regions are combined with the nominal $m_{\ell\ell\gamma\gamma}$ sideband, so that the signal region $115<m_{\ell\ell\gamma\gamma}<135~\GeV$ is excluded in all three cases. For each mass hypothesis the signal efficiency after the BDT working point is evaluated with each of the three reweightings applied to the signal, with the mass-hypothesis variable evaluated at the true ALP mass as in the training, and is compared with the efficiency obtained without reweighting. The largest upward and downward relative changes define an asymmetric log-normal uncertainty, \texttt{CMS\_hza\_mva\_reweight}, with one value per mass hypothesis obtained by combining the five eras weighted by their signal yields; mass points outside the generated grid take the value of the closest generated mass point. The nuisance parameter is fully correlated across eras and between the electron and muon channels. The downward variation $\kappa_{\mathrm{down}}$ lies between 0.91 and 1.00 and the upward variation $\kappa_{\mathrm{up}}$ between 1.00 and 1.06, for example $0.910/1.036$ at $m_{a}=1~\GeV$, $0.985/1.064$ at $m_{a}=5~\GeV$ and $0.945/1.000$ at $m_{a}=20~\GeV$. Averaged over the two directions, the effect on the signal yield is 1--6\%, as listed in Table~\ref{tab:uncertainty_summary}."""),
]
